Ferralk finds files. Glob matching and parallel filesystem walking, byte-first,
with an unsafe-free matcher.

[Explore Ferralk](https://ferralk.dev) or
[start with the Rust guide](https://ferralk.dev/guide/getting-started/).

Give it a glob such as `{src,packages}/**/*.{ts,tsx}` and it walks the tree in
parallel, applies `.gitignore` the way Git does, and opens only the directories
the pattern can reach. Filenames stay raw bytes and native `Path` values from
the first directory read to the last match, so nothing is lost to a UTF-8
conversion.

The [benchmark snapshot](#benchmark-snapshot) compares the walker and
matcher with other libraries on a warm repository-shaped fixture. It includes
both a search through every folder and a search restricted to named folders.

It is published as two crates, so a consumer that only needs matching does not
pay for traversal dependencies:

- `ferralk-glob` compiles a glob once and matches it against arbitrary bytes.
- `ferralk` walks filesystems with root-relative include and exclude patterns,
  and re-exports `ferralk-glob`.

Ferralk is independently developed and inspired by
[zlob 1.6.3](https://github.com/dmtrKovalenko/zlob). Its matcher and walker
behavior are checked against a frozen zlob reference, but it is not source
compatible with zlob and deliberately has no C ABI.

## Why Ferralk

- **The pattern prunes the walk.** `{src,packages}/**/*.{ts,tsx}` names the
  only roots that can match, so `node_modules` is never opened. Ferralk works
  that out from the pattern. With `ignore`, the same saving needs a
  hand-written `filter_entry`, and passing the globs as overrides alone still
  reads the whole tree. The [benchmark snapshot](#benchmark-snapshot) reports
both approaches on the same fixture.
- **Sized for the machine, not for the core count.** A warm walk is filesystem
  metadata work: profiled on macOS, 95% of it is `openat`, `getdirentries64`,
  and `close`, and a raw C walker issuing the same syscalls is no faster. What
  actually limits it is how much concurrency the kernel's namespace layer
  rewards, which on Apple Silicon is one performance cluster rather than every
  core. Ferralk defaults to that measured ceiling instead of
  `available_parallelism`, which is worth 1.7–1.9x out of the box on the test
  host; `Walker::threads` overrides it. The
  [evidence](docs/benchmark-evidence.md#the-default-worker-budget) records the
  controls, the one workload shape that wants more threads, and the hosts this
  was not measured on.
- **Git semantics come from Git.** Ignore verdicts are replayed against
  `git check-ignore` in CI, on Linux and on Windows, instead of being
  reimplemented from memory. Nested `.gitignore` chains, `.ignore` files,
  `.git/info/exclude`, linked worktrees, and `core.ignoreCase` are covered.
- **Nothing surprising is on by default.** Hidden files, symlink following,
  Git ignores, metadata, sorting, and error handling are explicit switches,
  and the [usage guide](docs/usage.md) documents each one with its default.
- **Safe Rust, with a named exception list.** The matcher crate forbids
  `unsafe` outright; the workspace denies it, so every remaining occurrence
  sits under an explicit `allow` in a file CI names with the reason it is
  there: the two opt-in native backends and their parity fixtures, one
  `sysctlbyname` query that sizes the default worker budget on Apple Silicon,
  and two counting allocators that exist only in test targets. A file that
  starts carrying `unsafe` fails CI, and so does a list entry that no longer
  needs to be on it.
- **Git is the oracle, not a spec someone read once.** The 1,123 checked-in
  behavioral cases are a machine-readable corpus rather than a side effect of
  the tests, and the ignore cases among them are replayed against
  `git check-ignore` itself, on Linux and on Windows, pinned to Git 2.52.0. If
  Git disagrees, CI fails; where Ferralk diverges on purpose, the case carries
  an ADR reference or a recorded oracle defect, and all 84 of them do.
- **Verification beyond a test suite.** Seven fuzz targets, differential checks
  against `fast-glob` and the frozen zlob reference, AddressSanitizer, Miri,
  and loom models of the scheduler protocol, across Linux, macOS, and Windows.
  [Verification depth](docs/verification-comparison.md#counts) counts what a
  pull request actually runs, and how the count was taken. The ADRs
  record the decisions rather than leaving them to be rediscovered. Wall-time
  walker benchmarks run on every pull request without gating; deterministic
  allocation-count tests and a Callgrind user-space CPU lane (gating at more
  than 2% over its merge base) do gate.

### Next to the crates you may already use

| Crate | What it is | Where Ferralk differs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [`ignore`](https://crates.io/crates/ignore) | ripgrep's parallel walker with Git-ignore support | Its overrides decide what is yielded, not what is opened, so pruning a subtree needs a hand-written `filter_entry`. Ferralk prunes from the include pattern itself. |
| [`globset`](https://crates.io/crates/globset) | Regex-backed matcher in which `*` crosses `/` by default | Ferralk's `*` stays inside one path component, as in a shell, and no pattern is translated to a regex. `WildcardMode::SeparatorCrossing` switches a walk to the `globset` reading when porting patterns. |
| [`walkdir`](https://crates.io/crates/walkdir) | Serial traversal with no selection of its own | Ferralk walks in parallel and selects while it walks. |
| [`zlob`](https://github.com/dmtrKovalenko/zlob) | Zig library behind a C ABI | Ferralk is an independent safe-Rust port of its matcher and walker semantics: no C ABI and no source compatibility. See the [migration guide](docs/compatibility-guide.md). |

## Status

Ferralk is stable. From 1.0.0 on, the
[1.x stability contract](docs/stability.md) is in force: it states which public
API, corpus semantics, entry-point rules, platform tier, and MSRV policy are
covered by semantic versioning, and which implementation details and feature
flags are not. The checked-in [`cargo public-api` listings](docs/api/) are the
API it covers, and a consumer-visible change to any of it is a major release.

1.0.0 settles the findings of the adversarial round run against
`1.0.0-rc.1`; the [compatibility guide](docs/compatibility-guide.md#contract-change-audit-since-090)
lists every behavior change since 0.9.0, including the ones 1.0.0 makes. Both
crates are published on crates.io; use the current
1.2.1 release line for applications. <!-- x-release-please-version -->

## Install

```toml
[dependencies]
ferralk = "1.2.1" # x-release-please-version
ferralk-glob = "1.2.1" # x-release-please-version
```

Depend on `ferralk-glob` alone when you only match strings or paths you already
hold. For an unreleased Git dependency, pin a revision for repeatable builds.

## Quick start

Compile a pattern once and match it many times. Syntax that changes meaning is
always opt-in: `PatternOptions::default()` reads `**`, braces, and extglobs as
ordinary text, and `PatternOptions::walker()` enables all three, exactly as the
walker does:

```rust
use ferralk_glob::{Pattern, PatternOptions};

let source_file = Pattern::compile("src/**/*.{rs,toml}", PatternOptions::walker())?;

assert!(source_file.is_match_glob_path("src/lib.rs"));
assert!(!source_file.is_match_glob_path("src/generated/lib.rs.bak"));
# Ok::<(), ferralk_glob::PatternError>(())
```

Walk a tree with the same component-aware pattern language:

```rust
use std::error::Error;

use ferralk::{ErrorPolicy, WalkOptions, Walker};

let result = Walker::new(".")
    .include("src/**/*.rs")?
    .exclude("**/generated/**")?
    .respect_git_ignore(true)
    .threads(4)
    .error_policy(ErrorPolicy::Collect)
    .options(WalkOptions::default().files_only(true).sort(true))
    .collect()?;

// `collect()?` succeeded, but that does not mean the walk did: under the
// default `ErrorPolicy::Collect` even a root that does not exist is reported
// in `errors()` below rather than as `Err`.
for entry in result.entries() {
    println!("{}", entry.path().display());
}

for error in result.errors() {
    // `Display` names the operation and path; `source()` says why it failed.
    match error.source() {
        Some(cause) => eprintln!("{error}: {cause}"),
        None => eprintln!("{error}"),
    }
}
# Ok::<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>(())
```

`collect()` sizes its workers to the host by default: available parallelism,
held to one performance cluster on Apple Silicon macOS. Use `threads(n)` to
choose a budget, or `threads(1)` for a single-threaded collection. Worker budgets are clamped to `1..=256` to bound
per-walk queues and operating-system threads. `stream()` is intentionally
single-threaded and unsorted so it can yield entries incrementally;
`stream_parallel()` yields them incrementally from the same workers `collect()`
uses, in no particular order.

For a runnable, Git-ignore-aware mini-find using both crates, try:

```sh
cargo run --example find -- 'src/**/*.rs'
```

The source is in [`crates/ferralk/examples/find.rs`](crates/ferralk/examples/find.rs).
The other [examples](crates/ferralk/examples) each run one recipe from the
crate documentation against a root you name, for instance
`cargo run -p ferralk --example gitignore_walk -- . '**/*.rs'`:
`gitignore_walk`, `include_exclude` (a fast-glob style list with `!`
excludes), `early_stop`, and `keep_going` (errors that do not end the walk).

### Four things worth knowing on day one

1. **Ordinary wildcards stay inside one path component.** `*.ts` selects
   `main.ts` in the walk root and not `src/main.ts`; write `**/*.ts` for
   descendants. The walker enables `**`, braces, and extglobs for you, while
   `ferralk-glob` leaves each one opt-in through `PatternOptions`;
   `PatternOptions::walker()` selects the walker's dialect in one call.
2. **Include wildcards do not cover a leading period.** `**/*.ts` skips
   `.cache/routes.ts` and the whole `.cache` subtree. Opt in with
   `Walker::match_hidden(true)`, or drop hidden entries before any pattern is
   consulted with `WalkOptions::skip_hidden(true)`. Excludes cover hidden
   names either way, as `.gitignore` lines do: `**/node_modules/**` also
   removes `.cache/node_modules`.
3. **Patterns use `/` on every platform, and `\` escapes.** Keep the root in
   `Walker::new(root)` and write the pattern relative to it. Joining a
   `PathBuf` into a pattern on Windows produces `\*`, which asks for a literal
   star.
4. **Nothing runs until you ask.** `.gitignore` is not read, symlinks below the
   root are not followed, metadata is not fetched, and entries are not sorted
   by default. Recoverable errors are collected next to the entries under the
   default `ErrorPolicy::Collect`, so a walk never silently returns less than
   it found.

The [usage guide](docs/usage.md) starts with a table of every default and the
switch that changes it.

## Benchmark snapshot

Measured on 2026-11-27 on a GitHub-hosted macOS 26.7.2 arm64 VM
(Apple M1, 3 vCPUs, 7 GiB RAM) at [`b42514d`](https://github.com/sebastian-software/ferralk/commit/b42514dcedbfc374bba581335fe7ef7469655562)
on `main`. The workspace version was 1.2.1. This revision includes changes
after the 1.2.1 release tag. Lower times are better.

On this runner, portable Ferralk took 33.87 ms for the unscoped query and
7.38 ms for the scoped query. In the same run, four-thread `jwalk` with
`globset` took 44.18 ms unscoped, and `ignore` with hand-written pruning took
21.41 ms scoped.

The zlob comparison uses 1.6.5. The frozen semantic reference still uses 1.6.3,
as recorded in [NOTICE](https://github.com/sebastian-software/ferralk/blob/main/NOTICE)
and [ADR-0007](docs/adr/0007-differential-corpus-and-dev-time-oracle.md).

The matcher uses `src/**/*.rs`. The Rust and zlob figures are Criterion
medians. The Node.js figures are medians of fifteen order-rotated samples,
each containing 100,000 matches.

| Matcher | Match | Non-match |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Ferralk (compiled) | 19 ns | 7 ns |
| zlob 1.6.5 (compiled) | 40 ns | 3 ns |
| `globset` (compiled) | 53 ns | 52 ns |
| `fast-glob` (interpreted Rust crate) | 195 ns | 205 ns |
| `wax` (compiled) | 51 ns | 65 ns |
| `picomatch` (compiled, Node.js) | 114.6 ns | 132.4 ns |
| `micromatch` (compiled, Node.js) | 100.1 ns | 125.8 ns |
| `minimatch` (compiled, Node.js) | 284.9 ns | 297.6 ns |

The walker fixture is a warm 53,601-file tree. The unscoped query
`**/*.{ts,tsx}` can match anywhere. The scoped query
`{src,packages}/**/*.{ts,tsx}` lets Ferralk skip folders outside its named
roots. The four-thread arms ran on the same 3-vCPU runner.

| Walker | Unscoped, 7,400 matches | Scoped, 2,600 matches |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Ferralk portable, 4 threads | 33.87 ms | 7.38 ms |
| `jwalk` + `globset`, 4 threads, same portable run | 44.18 ms | 53.17 ms |
| `ignore`, 4 threads, hand-pruned, same portable run | — | 21.41 ms |
| zlob 1.6.5, same portable run | 43.17 ms | 31.60 ms |
| Ferralk macOS-native, 4 threads | 35.31 ms | 5.49 ms |
| zlob 1.6.5, same native run | 27.67 ms | 35.34 ms |
| `node:fs` sync | 318.61 ms | 39.51 ms |
| `glob` async | 106.62 ms | 15.85 ms |
| `fast-glob` async | 145.29 ms | 25.16 ms |
| `tinyglobby` async | 84.93 ms | 10.51 ms |
| `fdir` + `picomatch` async | 78.91 ms | 70.92 ms |

The Rust walker rows are Criterion medians. The Node.js rows are medians of
ten order-rotated samples on the same runner. The APIs and estimators differ.
See the [benchmark evidence](docs/benchmark-evidence.md#current-library-comparison)
for the full macOS and Linux tables, commands, raw results, and limitations.
The [matched revision comparison](docs/benchmark-evidence.md#matched-revision-comparison)
measures the September revision, `v1.2.1`, and `main` on the same runners.

### Match against a list of globs

Ferralk's `PatternSet` and Rust's `globset::GlobSet` checked the same 64 paths
against 1,000 globs with indexable literals on the macOS runner above.
The sets were compiled before timing. The figures are per path.

| Workload | Ferralk `PatternSet` | `GlobSet` |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Check whether any glob matches | 47.4 ns | 93.0 ns |
| Report every matching glob | 43.9 ns | 331.4 ns |

The [full pattern-set comparison](docs/benchmark-evidence.md#pattern-sets)
covers shorter lists and patterns that Ferralk cannot index.

For measurements on real repositories, including `ignore` with hand-written
pruning, see [Palamedes adoption](docs/palamedes-adoption.md).

